            <ArticleTitle>Sickle cell disease of the spine in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>289-92</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the incidence of back pain in children admitted with sickle cell disease so as to increase awareness of this disease in black children presenting with back pain or discomfort.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A retrospective review.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">The Children's Hospital of Eastern Ontario in Ottawa, a tertiary care, university affiliated centre.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty children were treated for active sickle cell disease between 1990 and 1996. Eleven (5 boys, 6 girls) suffered vascular-occlusive phenomena occurring in bone, referred to as &quot;bony crises,&quot; requiring a total of 49 admissions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Clinical manifestations of spinal involvement by sickle cell disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The spine represented the second most common area of bone involvement (26%) exceeded only by the knee (35%). The vertebral level affected was lumbosacral in 66% of cases, followed by thoracic in 22% and cervical in only 12%. Eighty-six percent of the children with spinal pain were anemic upon presentation, 71% had an elevated leukocyte count, 15% were hyponatremic and 15% were hyperkalemic. Minimal physical signs in the spine were noted, other than a local tenderness over the spinous process in 71% and a decreased range of back motion in 17%.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Sickle cell disease is becoming more common in Canada as a result of increasing immigration from African countries and should always be considered as a possible cause of back pain in a black child.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
